      "id": 1,
      "text": "Palmer founded a New Knik post in the 1880s, turning the town into a gold‑rush supply hub served by dog sleds and wagons until the 1917 railroad bypass.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Knik-Fairview,_Alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Knik sits at the confluence of the Matanuska and Knik Rivers, giving Dena'ina hunters easy access to salmon, moose, and other resources.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Knik-Fairview,_Alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Dena'ina Athabascan people have lived near Knik since the 1500s, building villages and hunting, fishing, and gathering salmon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Knik-Fairview,_Alaska",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 2000, the community officially changed its name to Knik‑Fairview, becoming a census‑designated place.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knik-Fairview%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Community planning in Knik‑Fairview focuses on low‑density lots, protects Palmer Hay Flats and Goose Bay State Game Refuges, and supports recreation like dip‑netting in Fish Creek.",
